1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,103
One physical server, and ten applications.

2
00:00:03,653 --> 00:00:07,194
If they all share everything, one crash can take them all down.

3
00:00:07,744 --> 00:00:10,539
Virtualization and containers keep them apart.

4
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:15,057
Today: the problem, virtual machines, containers, and how they differ.

5
00:00:17,107 --> 00:00:18,344
First, the problem.

6
00:00:18,894 --> 00:00:22,798
A physical server is expensive, and it often sits mostly idle.

7
00:00:23,348 --> 00:00:27,700
Put every application on it, and they fight over the same libraries and settings.

8
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,856
So we need isolation: separate spaces on shared hardware.

9
00:00:33,906 --> 00:00:36,061
Solution one: the virtual machine.

10
00:00:36,611 --> 00:00:41,816
A hypervisor is software that splits one physical machine into several virtual ones.

11
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:46,697
Each virtual machine gets its own virtual processor, memory, and disk.

12
00:00:47,247 --> 00:00:50,617
And its own complete operating system, with its own kernel.

13
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:54,687
Strong isolation, but each one is heavy, and slower to start.

14
00:00:56,737 --> 00:00:58,721
One word matters here: the kernel.

15
00:00:59,271 --> 00:01:03,324
The kernel is the core of the operating system, and it talks to the hardware.

16
00:01:03,874 --> 00:01:06,072
A virtual machine has its own kernel.

17
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:09,011
Remember this, because it is the key difference.

18
00:01:11,061 --> 00:01:12,896
Solution two: the container.

19
00:01:13,446 --> 00:01:19,142
A container is an isolated process, with its own view of files, processes, and network.

20
00:01:19,692 --> 00:01:21,548
Containers share the host's kernel.

21
00:01:22,098 --> 00:01:24,743
So there is no extra operating system to boot.

22
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:26,178
They are light,

23
00:01:26,178 --> 00:01:27,771
and often start in seconds,

24
00:01:27,771 --> 00:01:30,840
but the isolation is thinner than a virtual machine.

25
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:34,085
Let us compare them.

26
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:38,645
A virtual machine has its own kernel. A container shares the host's.

27
00:01:39,195 --> 00:01:42,054
Virtual machines are heavier, and slower to start.

28
00:01:42,604 --> 00:01:44,631
Containers are lighter, and faster.

29
00:01:45,181 --> 00:01:49,405
Virtual machines isolate more strongly. Containers isolate less.

30
00:01:49,955 --> 00:01:52,621
Neither is better. They solve different problems.

31
00:01:54,671 --> 00:01:58,938
Now let us see isolation for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

32
00:01:59,488 --> 00:02:02,688
These outputs are real captures, not an illustration.

33
00:02:03,238 --> 00:02:08,550
First: this machine is called vm, and runs one kernel, version six point eighteen.

34
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:12,214
We count its running processes: sixty-seven lines.

35
00:02:12,764 --> 00:02:18,055
Now the unshare command starts a new, isolated space, and renames the host to box.

36
00:02:18,605 --> 00:02:20,510
Inside: the host name is box,

37
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:23,203
only four lines of processes are visible,

38
00:02:23,203 --> 00:02:25,240
and the kernel is the same one.

39
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:28,264
Back outside, the host name is still vm.

40
00:02:28,814 --> 00:02:33,486
Isolation, without a second kernel. That is the idea behind containers.

41
00:02:35,536 --> 00:02:37,243
These ideas are real tools.

42
00:02:37,793 --> 00:02:42,849
Virtual machines run on a hypervisor, such as KVM or VirtualBox.

43
00:02:43,399 --> 00:02:47,623
Containers are run by a container runtime, and Docker made them popular.

44
00:02:48,173 --> 00:02:51,671
Later in the bootcamp, we build and run containers ourselves.

45
00:02:53,721 --> 00:02:55,065
So when do you use which?

46
00:02:55,615 --> 00:03:00,266
Need a different operating system, or strong isolation? Use a virtual machine.

47
00:03:00,816 --> 00:03:04,762
Need many small, fast, identical services? Use containers.

48
00:03:05,312 --> 00:03:08,982
In the cloud, containers often run inside virtual machines.

49
00:03:09,532 --> 00:03:10,876
Choose by the problem.

50
00:03:12,926 --> 00:03:14,782
Watch out for four common myths.

51
00:03:15,332 --> 00:03:18,673
Myth one: a container is a small virtual machine.

52
00:03:18,673 --> 00:03:19,219
In fact,

53
00:03:19,219 --> 00:03:20,992
it is an isolated process,

54
00:03:20,992 --> 00:03:22,628
sharing the host kernel.

55
00:03:23,178 --> 00:03:26,055
Myth two: containers are always more secure.

56
00:03:26,055 --> 00:03:26,578
In fact,

57
00:03:26,578 --> 00:03:29,194
they isolate less than virtual machines.

58
00:03:29,744 --> 00:03:32,345
Myth three: virtual machines are obsolete.

59
00:03:32,345 --> 00:03:32,840
In fact,

60
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:34,079
they are everywhere,

61
00:03:34,079 --> 00:03:36,123
and containers often run on them.

62
00:03:36,673 --> 00:03:39,733
Myth four: Docker and a container are the same.

63
00:03:39,733 --> 00:03:40,254
In fact,

64
00:03:40,254 --> 00:03:42,859
Docker is one tool that runs containers.

65
00:03:43,409 --> 00:03:45,009
Know the difference, then choose.

66
00:03:47,059 --> 00:03:49,384
Here is how you check your own understanding.

67
00:03:49,934 --> 00:03:53,433
Imagine a colleague asks: virtual machine or container?

68
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:55,903
Explain why we need isolation.

69
00:03:56,453 --> 00:03:59,034
Explain a hypervisor, and a virtual machine.

70
00:03:59,584 --> 00:04:00,758
Explain the kernel.

71
00:04:01,308 --> 00:04:02,588
Explain a container.

72
00:04:03,138 --> 00:04:04,823
Compare the two on four points.

73
00:04:05,373 --> 00:04:07,741
Choose one for a scenario, and say why.

74
00:04:08,291 --> 00:04:09,912
To pass, you need all six.

75
00:04:11,962 --> 00:04:12,837
Your turn.

76
00:04:13,387 --> 00:04:18,571
On paper, draw a table with four rows: kernel, size, start time, and isolation.

77
00:04:19,121 --> 00:04:23,110
One: fill it in for virtual machines and for containers, from memory.

78
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:27,500
Two: imagine hosting a website made of twenty small services.

79
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:31,378
Three: choose one approach, and defend it in two sentences.

80
00:04:31,928 --> 00:04:33,528
Mark yourself on the rubric.

81
